{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>You are standing this day, all of you, before YHWH your Elohim.</b> Rashi explained: the most distinguished is counted first, and afterward all Israel. 'Your heads, your tribes' — meaning, according to your tribes. But in any case the lamed is missing, and not for nothing. Also, what did Moses see to arrange them in this particular order in this stance more than in all other assemblies when he gathered them?" "And the correct explanation is that he now wished to forge with them a new covenant, in place of the first covenant which was corrupted through the golden calf — which came about because they were not yet guarantors for one another, and therefore every man did what was right in his own eyes with none to say, 'Give it back!' Therefore he saw fit to bring them into the covenant-discipline of mutual surety by means of the oath at Mount Gerizim and Mount Ebal, for suretyship is a great cause for turning back many from sin, lest one be held liable for it — for every guarantor who sees the borrower squandering his money comes and rebukes him, out of fear lest he be required to pay on his behalf. So too when Israel are guarantors for one another, whoever has it in his power to rebuke, upon seeing an individual or a group veering from the path of reason, turns them back to the good. And if he closes his eyes to it, he is held in his place and held together with him in his lot, and the sin of the many hangs upon him. But as for one who lacks the power to rebuke, reason does not dictate that he be held liable for another's sin — for if not so, you would leave no creature alive, and it is certainly not on that assumption that they entered the covenant of suretyship. And if we find at times that the great ones are held liable even for the hidden sin of the generation, as happened in the sin of Achan, that is because before this they had not imposed their awe upon the public and had shut their eyes to transgressors — and after 'they that deal with it did not profit, they went their way' (Jeremiah 2:8), meaning that reproof would not avail them, so that through this cause every sinner and rebellious one grants himself a greeting of peace, thinking that the strap of rebellion has been loosened, and therefore he commits transgressions in batch after batch, sometimes openly and sometimes in secret — but if the great ones had always disciplined the rebellious people, they would have feared to sin even in private lest the matter become known. If so, it is only just that in every sin and guilt the hand of the officers is in the first degree, for every cause precedes what is caused." "Therefore it is said (Ezekiel 9:6), 'And from My sanctuary you shall begin,' and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted it (Shabbat 55a): from those who sanctify Me. And so they interpreted (Bava Kamma 60a) the verse, 'When a fire goes forth and finds thorns' — that punishment comes to the world only on account of the wicked, and it begins only from the righteous, and so forth. And the reason for this is that in their hands is the rod of rulers to turn back many from sin, yet their hands are slack from strengthening hands that are slack in Torah, and their knees buckle in faith. Therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Rosh HaShanah 8a): the king enters into judgment before the community, and so forth — for who is able and who is sensitive, besides him, to impose the fear of Heaven upon creatures, when in his hand is the rod and the lawgiver to establish his governance on earth. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tanhuma, Emor 22): on the eve of Rosh HaShanah the great ones go forth to greet him first, and afterward the intermediate ones, and so forth, and afterward all Israel on Yom Kippur — because every distinguished person who is distinguished more than his fellow enters into judgment first and gives his account first, since the guilt of the people hangs upon their heads." "Therefore Moses arranged them in the discipline of this covenant in this manner: since they were entering into suretyship, the great ones would always come first to give their account before those under them. And he placed the heads first — 'your heads, your tribes' — for the heads precede the elders everywhere, for one is always more heeded when he compels them to act with a firm hand, and a rod of discipline distances sin further than one who rebukes with speech alone, as it is written (Proverbs 29:19), 'By words a servant is not disciplined,' and it is said (ibid. 26:3), 'and a rod for the back of fools.' And even though 'a rebuke to the understanding from a single blow is more effective than a hundred against a fool' (ibid. 17:10), nevertheless with fools the measure is not so. And so it was always in Israel — a rod and a lawgiver, as it is written (Genesis 49:10), 'The scepter shall not depart from Judah, nor the lawgiver from between his feet' — 'scepter,' these are the exilarchs in Babylonia who rule the people with a rod; 'lawgiver,' these are the princes of the land of Israel who teach Torah to the multitudes. For this is the governance of kingship and the governance of the Sanhedrin — the one by the rod, and the others by the spirit of their lips. And since the sinner and the rebel obeys the one who rules with the rod more than he heeds the one who rebukes with the mouth — for he says, 'It is nothing but words' — therefore the king precedes them all to give account for the guilt of the people. Therefore it is said here too that your heads are at the head of the guarantors undertaking on behalf of all the people." "And he gave the reason for the matter: 'your heads' means 'your tribes' — they themselves are the rod that dominates you, as is found (Isaiah 10:5), 'Woe to Assyria, the rod of My anger' — he called Assyria 'a rod'; so too he called the heads 'a rod,' to say that they themselves are your tribes, and in their power to rebuke more than the elders. Therefore they precede in entering the suretyship. After them are all the upright of heart — the elders of Israel, lawgivers who with the spirit of their lips slay the wicked; and they too have officers who rule over the people, though not like the heads, for the heads have greater authority than the elders. And afterward every individual man of Israel precedes the women and children, for the women and children obey the men. And so too the convert is also subordinate to every individual man of Israel."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:14:47.700963494Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}